WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:02.905
The cloud bill doubled after launch.

2
00:00:02.925 --> 00:00:03.905
Cut the cost.

3
00:00:03.925 --> 00:00:07.165
But the nightly finance close must never slow down.

4
00:00:07.185 --> 00:00:13.340
Three ideas: how BigQuery charges, paying only for work, and guaranteed capacity.

5
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.125
First: BigQuery has two ways to pay.

6
00:00:16.145 --> 00:00:17.900
On-demand is like a taxi.

7
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.260
You pay for each trip: every byte your query scans.

8
00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:23.185
Editions are like renting a car.

9
00:00:23.205 --> 00:00:26.570
You pay for slots, which is compute, by the hour.

10
00:00:26.590 --> 00:00:27.695
Spiky, small use?

11
00:00:27.715 --> 00:00:28.595
Take the taxi.

12
00:00:28.615 --> 00:00:29.720
Heavy, steady use?

13
00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.570
Rent the car.

14
00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.605
Now, three guardrails that stop a runaway bill.

15
00:00:34.625 --> 00:00:36.918
A custom quota caps bytes per day.

16
00:00:36.938 --> 00:00:39.630
Maximum bytes billed stops one big query.

17
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:42.390
And a partition filter can be required.

18
00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:45.290
The trap: a budget alert does not stop spending.

19
00:00:45.310 --> 00:00:47.715
It only sends an email.

20
00:00:47.735 --> 00:00:50.650
Second: pay for compute only while it works.

21
00:00:50.670 --> 00:00:52.925
A Dataproc cluster that runs all day,

22
00:00:52.945 --> 00:00:56.835
for one nightly job, wastes money for 23 hours.

23
00:00:56.855 --> 00:00:58.547
So use a cluster per job.

24
00:00:58.567 --> 00:00:59.660
Add Spot workers.

25
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.270
Or go serverless.

26
00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:05.770
For Dataflow batch jobs that can wait, FlexRS is cheaper.

27
00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.130
Third: protect the finance close.

28
00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.780
A reservation is a fence around some slots.

29
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.090
Production gets its own fence.

30
00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.700
A baseline is the part that is always there, guaranteed.

31
00:01:16.720 --> 00:01:20.110
And an assignment says which project uses which fence.

32
00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.973
Idle slots are shared with others.

33
00:01:21.993 --> 00:01:24.360
So nothing is wasted.

34
00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:25.620
Recap.

35
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:27.658
Taxi or rental: know how you pay.

36
00:01:27.678 --> 00:01:28.620
Set guardrails.

37
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:30.530
A budget only alerts.

38
00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.330
Pay only while compute works.

39
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.065
And reserve slots for critical jobs.

40
00:01:35.085 --> 00:01:37.860
Try the checkpoint below.
